  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled weather expected through midweek as a \nfront meanders around the area. Turning noticeably cooler by \nWednesday.\n\nA cold front is currently moving south through the forecast\narea. Cooler, more stable air is filtering in behind the front\nand this can already be noted by stratus and NE winds already\nimpacting areas into the southern Piedmont of Virginia. West of\nthe Blue Ridge, atmosphere has sufficiently destabilized\nalready, and scattered strong storms have already begun to\ndevelop.\n\nStrong heating continues outside of the cool air wedging south,\nand a few locations across southern VA and northern NC may clip\n90F this afternoon. As storms continue to grow upscale and\ncongeal, a more widespread chance of rain continues into the\novernight. As far as heavy rain/localized flooding, there will \nbe a potential for that, with HREF probabilities for more than \n3\" rain in 12 hrs highlighting the Piedmont of NC tonight. Still\nmodels/ensembles are blotchy with this coverage and not seeing \na need for any flood watches, given the uncertainty and the\nongoing drought. However, there could still be some localized\nflooding issues where backbuilding occurs. \n\nDepending on rainfall that falls through tonight, another round\nof rain and storms tomorrow could pose another chance for some\nlocalized flooding, but again, not confident enough to pinpoint\nany particular region for a Flood Watch.\n\nThe bigger story will be the significant cool down with \npotential to remain stuck in the 50s and 60s Wednesday and \nThursday. Cold air advection is expected to persist for multiple\ndays so it may take until the weekend to return to readings near\nnormal. Wednesday and Thursday will likely feature overcast\nskies and some fog along the higher ridges with persistent\ndrizzle.",
